## 2. Component Specifications

### 2.1 Automated Temporal Incident-Date Safeguard Engine (Article 20(1) Enforcement)

#### Constitutional Logic Enforced:
* **Pre-July 1, 2024 Offences**: Substantive criminal liability **must strictly remain under the IPC 1860**. Applying BNS 2023 penal provisions retrospectively is an unconstitutional violation of **Article 20(1)** (*ex post facto law*).
* **Post-July 1, 2024 Offences**: Substantive criminal liability **must strictly be under BNS 2023**.
* **Hybrid Procedural Rule**: If an offence was committed before July 1, 2024, but the FIR, investigation, or trial is initiated on or after July 1, 2024:
  * **Substantive Law**: IPC 1860 (penal sections, compounding under 320 CrPC principles).
  * **Procedural Law**: BNSS 2023 (applies to prospective procedural steps, e.g., bail under Section 480/482/483 BNSS, subject to vested rights saved under Section 531(2)(a) BNSS).
  * **Evidentiary Law**: BSA 2023 vs Section 65B IEA depending on the creation and certificate generation timeline.

#### Engine Capabilities:
* **Date Parsing & Entity Extraction**: Detects date of occurrence/incident, date of complaint/FIR, and cited statutory provisions using high-precision regex + dateparser.
* **Violation Diagnostics**:
  1. `EX_POST_FACTO_PENAL_VIOLATION`: BNS section cited for pre-July 1, 2024 incident.
  2. `REPEALED_SUBSTANTIVE_LAW_CITED`: IPC section cited for post-July 1, 2024 incident.
  3. `PROCEDURAL_CONTINUITY_MISMATCH`: Pending proceeding initiated prior to cutoff citing post-cutoff procedures without invoking Section 531 BNSS savings clause.
* **Auto-Correction Payload**: Returns a clean replacement dictionary mapping defective sections to their legally required counterparts.

### 2.2 Verbatim Statutory Side-by-Side Diff Engine

#### Word-by-Word Diff Algorithm:
* Extracts the verbatim statutory text of legacy provisions (IPC/CrPC/IEA) and 2023 enactments (BNS/BNSS/BSA).
* Uses word-level sequence matching (`difflib.SequenceMatcher`) to compute exact lexical differences.
* Renders:
  * <span style="background-color: rgba(239, 68, 68, 0.2); color: #ef4444; text-decoration: line-through; padding: 2px 4px; border-radius: 4px;">Red Strikethrough</span>: Archaic colonial terminology, repealed phrases, deleted exceptions, or altered timeframes.
  * <span style="background-color: rgba(34, 197, 94, 0.2); color: #22c55e; font-weight: 600; padding: 2px 4px; border-radius: 4px;">Green Highlight</span>: Newly inserted words, modern mandatory obligations (e.g., electronic recording, forensic requirements), new penalties.
  * **Unchanged Text**: Retained in neutral Bookman/Inter serif typography showing *pari materia* continuity.

#### Procedural & Penal Metadata Delta:
For each compared section, displays a side-by-side comparison table:
| Parameter | Legacy Code (IPC / CrPC) | New Enactment (BNS / BNSS) | Litigator Impact |
| :--- | :--- | :--- | :--- |
| **Section Number** | Section 438 CrPC | Section 482 BNSS | Registry citation update |
| **Bailability** | Non-Bailable | Non-Bailable | Identical |
| **Triable By** | Court of Session / High Court | Court of Session / High Court | Retained |
| **Procedural Shift** | Discretionary conditions | Mandatory conditions + Notice to PP | Stricter notice requirement |
| **Sentencing Range** | Discretionary pre-arrest bail | Discretionary pre-arrest bail | Doctrine of Sibbia preserved |

### 2.3 Transitional Jurisprudence Live Feed

Curates and serves authoritative judicial rulings that interpret the transition interface:
* **Section 479 BNSS Undertrial Bail (Retrospective)**:
  * *Supreme Court of India* in *Re: Inhuman Conditions in 548 Prisons* — Held that the beneficial provision of Section 479 BNSS (bail for first-time offenders who have served 1/3rd sentence) applies retrospectively to pending undertrials.
* **Section 187 BNSS Police Custody Beyond 15 Days**:
  * Judicial divergence between High Courts on whether the 15-day custody can be taken in broken periods across the first 40 or 60 days.
* **Section 173(3) BNSS Preliminary Inquiry**:
  * Mandating 14-day inquiry for offences punishable between 3 to 7 years before FIR registration (*Lalita Kumari* doctrine modified).
* **Section 531 BNSS Savings Clause**:
  * Punjab & Haryana High Court and Delhi High Court decisions on maintainability of Section 482 CrPC quashing petitions filed after July 1, 2024 for pre-enactment FIRs.

Each feed item provides:
* Case Title, Official Citation, Bench, Decision Date
* Core Holding on Era Transition
* Court Pleading Paragraph ready for copy-pasting into bail/quashing petitions citing **Section 8 General Clauses Act**.

